### Symmetric Algorithms & Post-Quantum Extensions

#### AES-GCM-SIV for IPsec
- **Presentation**: [AES-GCM-SIV for IPsec](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-ipsecme-aes-gcm-siv-for-ipsec-00)
- **Presenter**: Casey Nguyen
- **Discussion**:
  - Proposed integrating AES-GCM-SIV (RFC 8452) into IKEv2 and ESP to provide inherent nonce misuse resistance.
  - Unlike AES-GCM, AES-GCM-SIV uses Polyval and requires a full pass over the plaintext to calculate the authentication tag before beginning counter-mode encryption.
  - The draft recommends transmitting a randomly generated 96-bit nonce and placing the authentication tag at the end of the Ciphertext field, leaving an empty ICV field.
  - Scott Fluhrer and chat participants noted that AES-NI / PCLMULQDQ hardware instructions handle Polyval and AES-GCM-SIV efficiently.
  - Tero Kivinen raised whether updating AES-GCM (RFC 4106) format definitions in parallel would be useful. Authors requested Working Group adoption.

#### Embedding Large PQC KEMs in IKEv2
- **Presentation**: [Embedding Large PQC KEMs in IKEv2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-ipsecme-embedding-large-pqc-kems-in-ikev2-00)
- **Presenter**: Daniel Harkins
- **Discussion**:
  - Evaluated eight transport and fragmentation mechanisms for large KEMs (ML-KEM, FrodoKEM, Classic McEliece) on OpenIKEv2 in a hardware testbed.
  - Standard RFC 9370 (`IKE_INTERMEDIATE`) and IKE fragmentation struggle with large keys (e.g., Classic McEliece > 1 MB) over lossy links due to static timers.
  - Results showed QUIC composite exchange and status-based selective acknowledgments perform best under high packet loss. Forward Error Correction (FEC via Raptor+/RaptorQ codes) effectively mitigates loss for medium/large KEMs without extra round trips.
  - For Classic McEliece, datagram approaches cause severe link congestion on 1 Mbps links, while TCP/QUIC throttle themselves due to loss being interpreted as congestion. Paul Wouters and Daniel Harkins noted that Classic McEliece is no longer prioritized by cryptographers, but serves as a useful benchmark data point.

#### IKE Fragmentation for Large Messages
- **Presentation**: [IKE Fragmentation for Large Messages](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-ipsecme-ike-fragmentation-for-large-messages-00)
- **Presenter**: Valery Smyslov
- **Draft**: `draft-smyslov-ipsecme-ike2-fragment-ack`
- **Discussion**:
  - Consolidates two previous proposals into a single joint specification to address fragmentation inefficiencies when transmitting large post-quantum signatures or KEM payloads (10+ KB) over standard IKE fragmentation (RFC 7383).
  - Version 02 introduces explicit negotiation using a new notification `FRAGMENT_ACK_SUPPORTED`. The receiver uses bitmaps or lists to report missing fragments so the sender only retransmits missing ones, reducing network congestion.
  - Valery requested Working Group adoption for the draft.

#### Merkle Tree Certificates in IKEv2
- **Presentation**: [Merkle Tree Certificates in the IKEv2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-ipsecme-merkle-tree-certificates-in-the-ikev2-00)
- **Presenter**: Valery Smyslov
- **Draft**: `draft-smyslov-ipsecme-ikev2-mtc`
- **Discussion**:
  - Proposed using Merkle Tree Certificates (MTC) to reduce authentication payload sizes by replacing public key signatures in identity certificates with CA log inclusion proofs.
  - Requires relying parties to periodically download updated landmark state.
  - Panos Kampanakis questioned the trade-off between periodic landmark retrieval overhead and sending 5 KB PQ certificates once per tunnel setup.
  - Tom Mijnbeek and John Gray noted that work in the PLANTS Working Group (`draft-gray-plants-mtc-ldp`) provides efficient subtree proof mechanisms so relying parties do not need full hourly fetches.
  - Christopher Patton and Paul Wouters noted that IPsec typically relies on private PKIs with 1–2 CAs rather than WebPKI with Certificate Transparency logs.

#### KEM-based Authentication for IKEv2
- **Presentation**: [KEM-based Authentication for IKEv2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-ipsecme-kem-based-authentication-for-ikev2-00)
- **Presenter**: Valery Smyslov (on behalf of Guilin Wang)
- **Discussion**:
  - Replaces ML-DSA signatures with ML-KEM key encapsulation for peer authentication in IKEv2.
  - Updated draft (-02) adds initiator identity protection against active attacks using derived keys and introduces an `Encrypted Certificate` (`E_CERT`) payload.
  - Quantitative analysis performed by Uri Blumenthal showed a 63% reduction in handshake computation work and a 44–64% reduction in wire byte size compared to ML-DSA.

